{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — Search results
  Phase 10. The page the header's search control has been pointing at since
  Phase 4.

  Sources of truth:
  - PHASE-1-WEBSITE-AUDIT.md SHOP-09, which lists the storefront routes with no
    template behind them. sections/main-cart.liquid answered the cart entry on
    that list in Phase 8 and named search as still outstanding; this is that
    entry.
  - PHASE-2-DESIGN-SYSTEM.md §9.3 (the product grid), §12 (forms), §10
    (buttons, and the one primary per band), §13 (the card).

  WHY THIS IS A BLOCKER AND NOT A FEATURE.
  sections/header.liquid renders its search control whenever show_search is on,
  and sections/header-group.json ships it on, so every page of the store
  carries a link to routes.search_url. Shopify serves its own error page for a
  storefront route whose template is missing, so until templates/search.json
  exists that control is a dead end on every page. The form is the deliverable;
  the results rendering is what makes the form worth pressing.

  SHOPIFY IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH.
  Nothing is ranked, weighted, filtered, boosted or excluded here. The results
  are whatever the storefront search endpoint returns for the term the customer
  typed, in the order it returns them. There is no synonym list, no "did you
  mean", no popular-searches row and no merchandised fallback, because each of
  those is either a business decision nobody has made or invented content.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE.
  No predictive search: it is out of Phase 10 scope, it needs a script and a
  second render target, and the plain form has to be correct first in any case
  — a predictive layer is an enhancement over this markup, not a replacement
  for it. No filters and no sort control: search.filters and search.sort_options
  are real, but a filter rail is a surface of its own and would arrive here
  half-built. No recently-searched list, which would need storage this theme
  does not have.

  ZERO JavaScript.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign search_types = section.settings.search_types
  assign per_page = section.settings.results_per_page

  assign cols_d = section.settings.columns_desktop
  assign cols_t = section.settings.columns_tablet
  assign cols_m = section.settings.columns_mobile

  assign surface_class = 'surface-light'
  if section.settings.surface == 'dark'
    assign surface_class = 'surface-dark'
  endif

  comment
    A search is only "performed" once q is in the URL, but q can be in the URL
    and empty — pressing the submit button with nothing typed does exactly
    that. Shopify still reports performed there, and rendering "0 results for
    """ is a worse answer than the invitation the page opens with, so the
    blank term falls back to the unsearched state.
  endcomment
  assign has_query = false
  if search.performed and search.terms != blank
    assign has_query = true
  endif

  assign has_results = false
  if has_query and search.results_count > 0
    assign has_results = true
  endif

  comment
    Group headings, and the heading level under them, come from search.types —
    what was actually searched — and not from what this page of results happens
    to contain. A page-by-page derivation would put the card titles at h3 on
    page one, where a page result appears alongside them, and at h2 on page two
    where it does not, so the same query would change its own outline as the
    customer paged through it. search.types is fixed for the whole query.
  endcomment
  assign group_headings = false
  if has_query and search.types.size > 1
    assign group_headings = true
  endif
  assign result_heading_level = 2
  if group_headings
    assign result_heading_level = 3
  endif

  comment
    The sizes attribute.

    The derivation is Phase 6's, in sections/featured-collection.liquid, minus
    the copy-column branches this section has no equivalent of. Its measurement
    is what rules out the obvious 100vw / count: the grid's column count is a
    CEILING, .product-grid floors every track at --product-col-min 272px, and
    where a viewport cannot carry the requested count the grid drops a column
    and the survivors stretch. Phase 6 measured two 292px tracks painted
    against a declared 196px at 1024 before that was accounted for. Each tier
    therefore emits two clauses: the requested count from the width at which it
    first fits, and one fewer below it.

    Chrome is the gutter on both sides, from the Phase 2 ladder — 24, 32 and 48
    per side.
  endcomment
  comment
    The sizes attribute is derived by snippets/grid-sizes.liquid, shared with
    the other grid that uses this exact arithmetic.

    It used to be ninety duplicated lines here. Phase 10 found that the desktop
    clause fired below its own breakpoint, booked it as a blocker, and fixed it
    in ONE of the copies; the other carried the bug for two more phases. Fixing
    an instance is not fixing a rule.
  endcomment
  assign chrome_d = 96
  capture sizes_attr
    render 'grid-sizes', cols_d: cols_d, cols_t: cols_t, cols_m: cols_m, chrome_d: chrome_d
  endcapture

  comment
    Built with append, not with "#{...}". Shopify's Liquid does not interpolate
    inside a string literal, and the Phase 10 header defect showed what that
    costs: a valid declaration carrying garbage.
  endcomment
  assign classes = 'main-search '
  assign classes = classes | append: surface_class
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  A template-bound section always renders, so its own stylesheet sits at the top
  unwrapped — the same shape as sections/main-cart.liquid.

  The card component's stylesheet does not, because the grid is conditional:
  the landing state of this page is a form and one line of copy, with not a
  single .product-card on it, and the card sheet is the largest in the theme.
  It is emitted inside the results branch instead, which is the reasoning
  Phase 6 applied to an unconfigured collection row.
{%- endcomment -%}
{{ 'section-main-search.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

<div class="{{ classes }}" {{ section.shopify_attributes }}>
  <div class="main-search__inner container">
    <h1 class="main-search__title">{{ 'sections.search.title' | t }}</h1>

    {%- comment -%}
      role="search" makes this a search landmark. The header's control is a
      link, not a form, so there is exactly one search landmark on the page and
      it does not need a distinguishing name.

      method="get", because a search is a request for a representation and its
      result has to be linkable, bookmarkable and reloadable. A POST here would
      make every result page unshareable and re-submit on back.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <form class="main-search__form" action="{{ routes.search_url }}" method="get" role="search">
      {%- comment -%}
        type is the documented contract for which object types the storefront
        search endpoint returns — product, page and article, comma separated.
        It defaults to ALL types, so without this input a merchant who chose
        Products only would still be served pages and articles, and the list
        below would contradict the setting that produced it.

        options[prefix]=last performs a partial word match on the final term, so
        a half-typed last word still matches: the documented example is "winter
        snow" reaching snowboard. Without it the last word has to be complete,
        which on a phone is the word most likely not to be.

        options[unavailable_products] is deliberately not sent. Its default is
        "last", which puts sold-out products after available ones — the order
        this page wants — and snippets/product-card.liquid already renders the
        sold-out state as text on the tile. Sending "hide" instead would be a
        merchandising decision, and it is not one this phase gets to make.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <input type="hidden" name="type" value="{{ search_types }}">
      <input type="hidden" name="options[prefix]" value="last">

      {%- comment -%}
        A visible label, not a placeholder. Phase 2 §12.6 forbids
        placeholder-only labels outright and §12.3's filled row requires the
        label to stay visible above the field: placeholder text disappears at
        the moment the customer starts typing, which is the moment it was there
        to help with. No placeholder is set either, because one that repeated
        this label would only add noise.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <label class="main-search__label" for="SearchInput">
        {{ 'sections.search.input_label' | t }}
      </label>

      <div class="main-search__field">
        {%- comment -%}
          escape, not raw. search.terms is customer input echoed straight back
          out, and an attribute value is the one place on this page where
          nothing else escapes it for us.

          The two | t calls below pass search.terms unfiltered instead.
          Shopify's storefront locale documentation states that translated
          content is escaped by default — every HTML character is converted to
          its entity equivalent — and the only opt-out is the _html key suffix,
          which neither of those keys carries. Escaping the argument as well
          would print the entities rather than the customer's words.

          No autocomplete attribute. §12.4 asks for one on every field, and its
          examples are the autofill tokens — name, given-name, address-line1.
          There is no autofill token for a site search term, and
          autocomplete="off" would only suppress the browser's own history for
          this field, which is the wrong trade on the one field a returning
          customer is most likely to retype.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <input
          class="main-search__input"
          id="SearchInput"
          type="search"
          name="q"
          value="{{ search.terms | escape }}"
        >

        {%- comment -%}
          No width or height rule of its own: a grid item stretches to its
          column, so the button runs the full width of the stacked layout below
          --bp-md and matches the field's 48px on the row above it.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <button class="button button--primary" type="submit">
          {% render 'icon-search' %}
          {{ 'sections.search.submit' | t }}
        </button>
      </div>
    </form>

    {%- if has_query == false -%}
      <p class="main-search__prompt">{{ 'sections.search.prompt' | t }}</p>

    {%- elsif has_results == false -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        A zero-result page says the term back, so the customer can see what was
        actually searched — a typo is invisible once the field has scrolled out
        of view — and then says what to try, which is generic advice about
        spelling and word length. It does not apologise, recommend anything, or
        offer a "did you mean": there is no data behind a suggestion and an
        invented one would send people to the wrong product.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p class="main-search__empty-title">
        {{ 'sections.search.no_results' | t: terms: search.terms }}
      </p>
      <p class="main-search__empty-body">{{ 'sections.search.no_results_help' | t }}</p>

    {%- else -%}
      {{ 'component-product-card.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

      {%- comment -%}
        The column counts are merchant settings, so they arrive as scoped custom
        properties rather than as inline style on the grid: an inline
        declaration would outrank every media query and pin the mobile count to
        all three tiers.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {% style %}
        #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .product-grid { --product-cols: {{ cols_m }}; }
        @media (min-width: 768px) {
          #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .product-grid { --product-cols: {{ cols_t }}; }
        }
        @media (min-width: 1024px) {
          #shopify-section-{{ section.id }} .product-grid { --product-cols: {{ cols_d }}; }
        }
      {% endstyle %}

      <p class="main-search__summary">
        {{- 'sections.search.results_count' | t: count: search.results_count, terms: search.terms -}}
      </p>

      {%- paginate search.results by per_page -%}
        {%- liquid
          comment
            search.results is a mixed array — products, pages and articles in
            relevance order — and paginate slices it without regard to type, so
            any one page can carry any mix. A product belongs in the product
            grid and a page does not, so the slice is rendered twice, each pass
            taking only its own type. Counting first is what keeps an empty
            <ul> or an empty group heading off the page when a slice happens to
            be all of one kind.
          endcomment
          assign product_results = 0
          assign other_results = 0
          for item in search.results
            if item.object_type == 'product'
              assign product_results = product_results | plus: 1
            else
              assign other_results = other_results | plus: 1
            endif
          endfor
        -%}

        {%- if product_results > 0 -%}
          <div class="main-search__group">
            {%- if group_headings -%}
              <h2 class="main-search__group-heading">{{ 'sections.search.products' | t }}</h2>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- comment -%}
              forloop.first is the wrong test for the eager tile here. The loop
              runs over the mixed slice, so on a query whose top result is a
              page it would be true for that page and no product would ever be
              loaded eagerly — leaving the tile most likely to be the largest
              contentful paint behind a lazy fetch. The count of tiles actually
              emitted is the test instead.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- assign tiles_emitted = 0 -%}
            <ul class="product-grid" role="list">
              {%- for item in search.results -%}
                {%- if item.object_type == 'product' -%}
                  {%- assign tiles_emitted = tiles_emitted | plus: 1 -%}
                  {%- assign eager_tile = false -%}
                  {%- if tiles_emitted == 1 -%}
                    {%- assign eager_tile = true -%}
                  {%- endif -%}
                  <li class="product-grid__item">
                    {% render 'product-card',
                      product: item,
                      heading_level: result_heading_level,
                      sizes: sizes_attr,
                      eager: eager_tile
                    %}
                  </li>
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endfor -%}
            </ul>
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- if other_results > 0 -%}
          <div class="main-search__group">
            {%- if group_headings -%}
              <h2 class="main-search__group-heading">{{ 'sections.search.other_results' | t }}</h2>
            {%- endif -%}

            <ul class="main-search__other-list" role="list">
              {%- for item in search.results -%}
                {%- unless item.object_type == 'product' -%}
                  <li class="main-search__other">
                    {%- comment -%}
                      The type label is text, not an icon and not a colour: a
                      customer has to be able to tell a policy page from a
                      journal entry before they press it.
                    {%- endcomment -%}
                    <p class="main-search__other-type">
                      {%- if item.object_type == 'article' -%}
                        {{ 'sections.search.result_type.article' | t }}
                      {%- else -%}
                        {{ 'sections.search.result_type.page' | t }}
                      {%- endif -%}
                    </p>

                    <h{{ result_heading_level }} class="main-search__other-title">
                      <a class="main-search__other-link" href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.title }}</a>
                    </h{{ result_heading_level }}>

                    {%- comment -%}
                      An excerpt for articles only. article.excerpt_or_content is
                      a field the merchant writes for exactly this purpose. A
                      page has no equivalent — truncating page.content would
                      print the first twenty-five words of a shipping policy
                      under its own title, which tells a customer nothing they
                      could not read from the title.
                    {%- endcomment -%}
                    {%- if item.object_type == 'article' and item.excerpt_or_content != blank -%}
                      <p class="main-search__other-excerpt">
                        {{- item.excerpt_or_content | strip_html | truncatewords: 25 -}}
                      </p>
                    {%- endif -%}
                  </li>
                {%- endunless -%}
              {%- endfor -%}
            </ul>
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- if paginate.pages > 1 -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            Built by hand rather than with default_pagination. That filter emits
            its own markup and its own English — its previous and next links are
            guillemet entities plus the words — and no locale file can reach any
            of it. This theme's rule is that every storefront-visible string is a
            | t lookup, so the filter cannot be used here at all. Building the
            nav also puts rel, aria-current and the accessible name of each
            numbered link under this file's control rather than the filter's.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            The label is assigned first. Shopify rejects a filter on a render
            argument outright — "Filters cannot be used on arguments passed to
            the render tag" — and the Mini-Liquid harness does not model that
            rule, so it rendered happily and Theme Check is what caught it.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- assign pagination_label = 'sections.search.pagination' | t -%}
          {% render 'pagination', paginate: paginate, label: pagination_label %}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endpaginate -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Search results",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "main-search-section",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Search"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "search_types",
      "label": "What search looks through",
      "info": "Products always come back as a grid of product cards. Pages and articles come back as a titled list below it. Everything searched is your own Shopify content; nothing is set in the theme.",
      "options": [
        { "value": "product", "label": "Products only" },
        { "value": "product,page,article", "label": "Products, pages and articles" }
      ],
      "default": "product"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "results_per_page",
      "label": "Results per page",
      "min": 12,
      "max": 48,
      "step": 4,
      "default": 24,
      "info": "A count that divides evenly into the desktop column count fills its rows."
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Layout"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "surface",
      "label": "Colour scheme",
      "info": "The theme switches text, border, accent and focus colours to suit the band. Muted gold is a dark-surface accent; on cream a darker gold is substituted automatically.",
      "options": [
        { "value": "light", "label": "Cream" },
        { "value": "dark", "label": "Ink" }
      ],
      "default": "light"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Grid"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "These are maximums. A tile never drops below a readable width, so if a viewport cannot carry the count you choose, the grid drops a column rather than squeezing one."
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "columns_desktop",
      "label": "Columns, desktop",
      "min": 2,
      "max": 4,
      "step": 1,
      "default": 4
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "columns_tablet",
      "label": "Columns, tablet",
      "min": 1,
      "max": 3,
      "step": 1,
      "default": 2
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "columns_mobile",
      "label": "Columns, mobile",
      "min": 1,
      "max": 3,
      "step": 1,
      "default": 2
    }
  ],
  "enabled_on": {
    "templates": ["search"]
  }
}
{% endschema %}
